  <!-- ============ CASE STUDY 1: ZERO TO ONE, THE IN-HOUSE PAYMENT GATEWAY ============ -->
  <article data-view="case-1" hidden class="case">
    <header class="w ch"><a class="back" href="#work" data-go="home" data-anchor="work">← Selected work</a><span class="tag">Zero to one · Payment gateway, 2021–2022</span>
      <h1 class="grad">An in-house payment gateway, live in under a year</h1>
      <p class="lede">The company was paying too much for third-party payment vendors it couldn’t fully trust with custody or compliance. I designed an in-house gateway, hired the engineers, and took it live in November 2021. In its first full year, payment costs fell 68%.</p>
      <dl class="chips">
        <div>
          <dt>Role</dt>
          <dd>Senior Product Manager (Head of Blockchain)</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Team</dt>
          <dd>4 engineers I hired</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Duration</dt>
          <dd>2021 – 2022</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Company</dt>
          <dd>OG Global</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </header>
    <section class="w">
      <ul class="kpis">
        <li><b>68%</b><span>lower payment costs, first full year live (2022)</span><em>Primary</em></li>
        <li><b>Nov 2021</b><span>live, the same year the work began</span><em>Speed</em></li>
        <li><b>4</b><span>engineers, hired from zero</span><em>Team</em></li>
        <li><b>1 core</b><span>custody, screening, ledger and approvals, built once</span><em>Foundation</em></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="fine dnote">Draft note: approximate figures from Drake’s own account; definitions, sources and approved wording pending (metric sheet).</p>
    </section>
    <div class="w cgrid">
      <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <p>On this page</p>
        <a href="#a1-context">Context</a><a href="#a1-goals">Goals and measures</a><a href="#a1-role">Role and constraints</a><a href="#a1-architecture">How it fits together</a><a href="#a1-options">Options considered</a><a href="#a1-decisions">Key decisions</a><a href="#a1-tradeoffs">Trade-offs</a><a href="#a1-execution">How it shipped</a><a href="#a1-lessons">What I’d carry forward</a>
      </nav>
      <div class="prose">
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a1-context" data-sec>
          <h2>Context</h2>
          <p>Payments ran through third-party vendors. They cost too much, and the company couldn’t fully trust them with custody of funds or with the compliance checks it needed. Leadership wanted an in-house solution.</p>
          <p>There was no product, no team and no code: a direction, and the freedom to hire. The brief fit in a sentence: “Bring crypto payments in-house: lower cost, our own custody, our own compliance checks.”</p>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a1-goals" data-sec>
          <h2>Goals and measures</h2>
          <ul class="cs-list">
            <li>Replace third-party payment vendors with a gateway the company controls</li>
            <li>Keep custody of funds and compliance checks in-house</li>
            <li>Lower the cost of every payment</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="cs-tw">
            <table class="cs-tbl">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Measure</th>
                  <th scope="col">Role</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>Payment costs compared with the vendors</td>
                  <td><span class="cs-pill k-primary">Primary</span></td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a1-role" data-sec>
          <h2>Role and constraints</h2>
          <p>I owned the product end to end. I designed the solution, got buy-in from the stakeholders, hired the engineering team, and finalized the design with them. I owned the division’s budget and vendor decisions, and reported to the CTO.</p>
          <ul class="cs-list">
            <li>A small team: four engineers, so one core instead of separate codebases</li>
            <li>Other people’s money: every movement needed screening, approvals and an audit trail</li>
            <li>An admin console had to be built alongside the gateway to run it</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a1-architecture" data-sec>
          <h2>How it fits together</h2>
          <p>Three principles came out of the team’s size, and everything built later followed them: one core, many products; configuration over custom code; and controls are product.</p>
          <figure class="art cs-art">
            <div class="mk" role="img" aria-label="At launch: the payment gateway and its admin console on one shared core of custody, screening, ledger and approvals">
              <div class="mk-bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>the gateway at launch</span></div>
              <div class="mk-arch">
                <div class="mk-row prod"><span class="mk-node up"><span class="nl">Payment Gateway</span><span class="ns">Gateway</span></span><span class="mk-node up"><span class="nl">Admin console</span><span class="ns">Console</span></span></div>
                <div class="mk-core"><b>Shared core</b><span>Custody · screening · ledger · approvals</span><i class="mk-pulse"></i></div>
                <div class="mk-row nets"><span class="mk-node dn"><span class="nl">Blockchain networks</span><span class="ns">Networks</span></span></div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <figcaption>At launch: the gateway and its admin console on one core. Everything added later was built on the same core.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a1-options" data-sec>
          <h2>Options considered</h2>
          <div class="cs-opts">
            <div class="cs-opt v-rejected"><span class="cs-v"><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4.5 4.5l7 7m0-7l-7 7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>Rejected</span><b>Stay with third-party vendors</b>
              <p>Costs kept rising, and custody and compliance stayed outside our control.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="cs-opt v-chosen"><span class="cs-v"><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3.5 8.5l3 3 6-7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Chosen</span><b>Build an in-house gateway on one shared core</b>
              <p>Full control of custody, screening and cost, with a team small enough to move fast.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a1-decisions" data-sec>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <div class="cs-decs">
            <div class="cs-dec"><span>Decision 1</span><b>Build the core once</b>
              <p>Custody, screening, the ledger and approvals were built once, so anything added later would be a client of the core, never a fork of it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="cs-dec"><span>Decision 2</span><b>Keep screened funds apart</b>
              <p>Clean, higher-risk and failed-screening funds each get their own balances and wallets, so they can’t be mixed by mistake.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="cs-dec"><span>Decision 3</span><b>Tell the client twice</b>
              <p>One callback when a payment appears, another once it’s confirmed, screened and credited: one for the customer, one for the books.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <blockquote class="cs-pull">Build the boring core once, and make every product a client of it.</blockquote>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a1-tradeoffs" data-sec>
          <h2>Trade-offs</h2>
          <div class="cs-trades">
            <div class="cs-trade"><b>Separate balances for each risk class</b><span>over</span><s>One balance with a “flagged” column</s>
              <p>A single balance is how clean-looking money gets spent on a bad day. Separate balances make that mistake impossible, not just unlikely.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="cs-trade"><b>Two callbacks</b><span>over</span><s>One simpler callback</s>
              <p>The first spares the customer a spinner; the second makes sure the client never credits money that hasn’t cleared.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a1-execution" data-sec>
          <h2>How it shipped</h2>
          <ol class="cs-steps">
            <li><span>2021</span>Designed the solution and got stakeholder buy-in</li>
            <li><span>2021</span>Hired four engineers and finalized the design with them</li>
            <li><span>2021</span>Built the gateway and its admin console on one core</li>
            <li><span>Nov 2021</span>Live</li>
          </ol>
          <p>In 2022, its first full year live, payment costs came in 68% lower than with the vendors it replaced.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a1-lessons" data-sec>
          <h2>What I’d carry forward</h2>
          <ul class="cs-list">
            <li>A small team forces good architecture. Four engineers can’t maintain separate codebases, so we built one core.</li>
            <li>Separate balances beat careful people. Make mixing flagged and clean funds impossible, not just unlikely.</li>
            <li>Design the exits first. Refunds, rejections and failed transfers need an owner and an audit trail before the first deposit.</li>
            <li>Network fees are a product problem. Getting caught out by them on TRON is what led to energy rental and smart withdrawal routing.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
