  <!-- ============ CASE STUDY 2: ONE TO 100, FROM PAYMENT GATEWAY TO PAYMENTS PLATFORM ============ -->
  <article data-view="case-2" hidden class="case">
    <header class="w ch"><a class="back" href="#work" data-go="home" data-anchor="work">← Selected work</a><span class="tag">One to 100 · Payments platform, 2022–2026</span>
      <h1 class="grad">From payment gateway to payments platform</h1>
      <p class="lede">Once the gateway worked for us, we opened it to outside businesses as a B2B payments platform, and my team and I built a consumer wallet and a treasury product on the same core. It grew from two internal clients to about 20, and to more than US$1 billion a year in transaction volume.</p>
      <dl class="chips">
        <div>
          <dt>Role</dt>
          <dd>Senior Product Manager (Head of Blockchain)</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Team</dt>
          <dd>Blockchain division, 4–14 people over time</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Duration</dt>
          <dd>2022 – 2026</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Company</dt>
          <dd>OG Global</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </header>
    <section class="w">
      <ul class="kpis">
        <li><b>$1B+</b><span>annual transaction volume at full scale</span><em>Primary</em></li>
        <li><b>2 → about 20</b><span>clients using the platform</span><em>Growth</em></li>
        <li><b>4</b><span>products on one shared core</span><em>Scope</em></li>
        <li><b>+8%</b><span>of new wallet customers verified and made a first deposit</span><em>Onboarding</em></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="fine dnote">Draft note: approximate figures from Drake’s own account; definitions, sources and approved wording pending (metric sheet).</p>
    </section>
    <div class="w cgrid">
      <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <p>On this page</p>
        <a href="#a2-context">Context</a><a href="#a2-goals">Goals and measures</a><a href="#a2-role">Role and constraints</a><a href="#a2-architecture">How it fits together</a><a href="#a2-options">Options considered</a><a href="#a2-decisions">Key decisions</a><a href="#a2-tradeoffs">Trade-offs</a><a href="#a2-onboarding">Scaling the wallet: onboarding</a><a href="#a2-execution">How it shipped</a><a href="#a2-lessons">What I’d carry forward</a>
      </nav>
      <div class="prose">
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a2-context" data-sec>
          <h2>Context</h2>
          <p>Once the gateway was live, it served two internal clients. The infrastructure already existed, so the question was what else it could become.</p>
          <p>Some clients didn’t want to build their own product on our API; they wanted something turnkey. And our own treasury team, like several clients, was still moving funds for settlements and payouts by hand.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a2-goals" data-sec>
          <h2>Goals and measures</h2>
          <ul class="cs-list">
            <li>Turn the gateway into a B2B payments platform and a new revenue stream</li>
            <li>Give clients a turnkey option: a white-label consumer wallet</li>
            <li>Give operations teams a safe way to hold and move funds, with approvals and records</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="cs-tw">
            <table class="cs-tbl">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Measure</th>
                  <th scope="col">Role</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>Clients using the platform</td>
                  <td><span class="cs-pill k-primary">Primary</span></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Annual transaction volume</td>
                  <td><span class="cs-pill k-secondary">Secondary</span></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Onboarding completion: identity checks and a first deposit</td>
                  <td><span class="cs-pill k-secondary">Secondary</span></td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a2-role" data-sec>
          <h2>Role and constraints</h2>
          <p>Product ownership was mine: the roadmap, the design of every product and the trade-offs. Opening the gateway to outside clients needed the CTO, the COO and the rest of the leadership team to agree and fund it, and sales to find the clients.</p>
          <ul class="cs-list">
            <li>One admin console, shared safely between our own operators and many client teams</li>
            <li>Responsible for compliance for other businesses’ customers, not just our own</li>
            <li>One team, splitting its time between internal needs and outside clients</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a2-architecture" data-sec>
          <h2>How it fits together</h2>
          <p>The wallet is a client of the gateway, like any business that integrates with us, so it gets the same custody, screening and approval controls. Products are configured, not forked: assets, payment methods, limits and fees are settings at platform, client and brand level.</p>
          <figure class="art cs-art">
            <div class="mk" role="img" aria-label="Platform map: four products on one shared core, connected to five networks">
              <div class="mk-bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>the platform map</span></div>
              <div class="mk-arch">
                <div class="mk-row prod"><span class="mk-node up"><span class="nl">Payment Gateway</span><span class="ns">Gateway</span></span><span class="mk-node up"><span class="nl">Consumer Wallet</span><span class="ns">Wallet</span></span><span class="mk-node up">Treasury</span><span class="mk-node up"><span class="nl">Admin console</span><span class="ns">Console</span></span></div>
                <div class="mk-core"><b>Shared core</b><span>Custody and wallets · transaction screening · ledger · approvals and permissions</span><i class="mk-pulse"></i></div>
                <div class="mk-row nets"><span class="mk-node dn">TRON</span><span class="mk-node dn"><span class="nl">Ethereum</span><span class="ns">ETH</span></span><span class="mk-node dn"><span class="nl">BNB Smart Chain</span><span class="ns">BSC</span></span><span class="mk-node dn"><span class="nl">Solana</span><span class="ns">SOL</span></span><span class="mk-node dn"><span class="nl">Bitcoin</span><span class="ns">BTC</span></span></div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <figcaption>Four products, one core. The consumer wallet is itself a client of the payment gateway.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a2-options" data-sec>
          <h2>Options considered</h2>
          <div class="cs-opts">
            <div class="cs-opt v-rejected"><span class="cs-v"><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4.5 4.5l7 7m0-7l-7 7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>Rejected</span><b>Keep the gateway internal</b>
              <p>Build only for our own needs, with no outside clients to support.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="cs-opt v-chosen"><span class="cs-v"><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3.5 8.5l3 3 6-7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Chosen</span><b>Open it to outside clients as a B2B platform</b>
              <p>The infrastructure already existed, so it could earn revenue and grow the team.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a2-decisions" data-sec>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <div class="cs-decs">
            <div class="cs-dec"><span>Decision 1</span><b>Take the gateway to market</b>
              <p>With the CTO, the COO and sales, turned an internal tool into a B2B product and a new revenue stream. Product ownership stayed with me.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="cs-dec"><span>Decision 2</span><b>Make the wallet a client of the gateway</b>
              <p>Our own consumer app went through the same controls as any client, so we felt every rough edge before a client did.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="cs-dec"><span>Decision 3</span><b>Test onboarding before switching</b>
              <p>Ran automated identity checks from two vendors against the old manual review, chose the winner on the results, and got compliance sign-off.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <blockquote class="cs-pull">Quoting a new client was a settings change, not a sprint.</blockquote>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a2-tradeoffs" data-sec>
          <h2>Trade-offs</h2>
          <div class="cs-trades">
            <div class="cs-trade"><b>New revenue from outside clients</b><span>over</span><s>A team focused only on internal needs</s>
              <p>It meant rebuilding the admin console as multi-tenant, splitting the team’s time, and taking on support and compliance responsibility for other businesses’ customers.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="cs-trade"><b>Configuration</b><span>over</span><s>Custom builds for each client</s>
              <p>Assets, payment methods, limits and fees are settings, so new clients get onboarded, not built for.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="cs-trade"><b>Vendor capability</b><span>over</span><s>Building identity checks ourselves</s>
              <p>Specialist vendors could verify customers better than we could have built in-house.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a2-onboarding" data-sec>
          <h2>Scaling the wallet: onboarding</h2>
          <p>Onboarding was manual: customers entered their details, uploaded documents and waited for a person to review them. Our B2B partners needed a better onboarding and compliance flow for the white-label wallet to succeed.</p>
          <p>I chose two identity-verification vendors to test, designed an A/B test against the old flow and what counted as success, picked the better vendor on the results, and took them to compliance, who reviewed the vendor and signed off. People still reviewed anything unusual.</p>
          <p>The share of new customers who completed their identity checks and made a first deposit rose 8% against the old manual flow.</p>
          <figure class="art cs-art">
            <div class="mk" role="img" aria-label="The automated onboarding flow: identity and business checks pass automatically, an unusual case goes to a person, then the first deposit">
              <div class="mk-bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>onboarding — variant B</span></div>
              <div class="mk-ap">
                <div class="mk-head"><b>New customer</b><span class="mk-chip">Automated checks</span><span class="mk-fil">A/B test</span></div>
                <div class="mk-arow"><span class="mk-av lg">1</span>
                  <div><b>Identity check (KYC)</b><span>Verified <i class="">Automatic</i></span></div>
                  <span class="mk-btn sm">Done</span>
                </div>
                <div class="mk-arow"><span class="mk-av lg">2</span>
                  <div><b>Business check (KYB)</b><span>Verified <i class="">Automatic</i></span></div>
                  <span class="mk-btn sm">Done</span>
                </div>
                <div class="mk-arow"><span class="mk-av lg">3</span>
                  <div><b>Unusual case</b><span>Flagged <i class="warn">Needs a person</i></span></div>
                  <span class="mk-btn sm">Review</span>
                </div>
                <div class="mk-arow"><span class="mk-av lg">4</span>
                  <div><b>First deposit</b><span>Received <i class="">Onboarded</i></span></div>
                  <span class="mk-btn sm">Done</span>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <figcaption>The new flow: identity and business checks run automatically, and anything unusual goes to a person.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a2-execution" data-sec>
          <h2>How it shipped</h2>
          <ol class="cs-steps">
            <li><span>2022</span>B2B direct API and a multi-tenant console for outside merchants</li>
            <li><span>Next</span>More blockchains and tokens</li>
            <li><span>Late 2022 – 2023</span>Consumer wallet, live in 2023</li>
            <li><span>Late 2023</span>Treasury: custody and payouts with layered approvals</li>
            <li><span>2024</span>Onboarding and identity checks automated, after an A/B test</li>
          </ol>
          <p>Until 2026, the team kept building on the core: TRON energy rental and a peer-to-peer exchange in the wallet, and steady improvements to the gateway and back-end transaction flows.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a2-lessons" data-sec>
          <h2>What I’d carry forward</h2>
          <ul class="cs-list">
            <li>Design for outside clients from day one. We built the console for ourselves first, then had to rebuild it as multi-tenant.</li>
            <li>Be your own customer. Running our wallet on the gateway gave us a live reference for every sales call.</li>
            <li>Test vendors side by side on real users, not on their pitch.</li>
            <li>Bring compliance the data, not a request. The results made the approval easy.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
