  <!-- ============ CASE STUDY 3: ONE API FOR EVERY PAYMENT PROVIDER ============ -->
  <article data-view="case-3" hidden class="case">
    <header class="w ch"><a class="back" href="#work" data-go="home" data-anchor="work">← Selected work</a><span class="tag">Zero to one, then one to 100 · Payment orchestration, 2023 onward</span>
      <h1 class="grad">One API for every payment provider</h1>
      <p class="lede">Every payment provider came with its own integration, so each team and client system integrated with each one separately. I designed an orchestration layer with one API. It launched with our own gateway and two outside payment service providers (PSPs), then grew to more than 40.</p>
      <dl class="chips">
        <div>
          <dt>Role</dt>
          <dd>Senior Product Manager (Head of Blockchain)</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Team</dt>
          <dd>Blockchain division, 4–14 people over time</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Duration</dt>
          <dd>2023 onward</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Company</dt>
          <dd>OG Global</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </header>
    <section class="w">
      <ul class="kpis">
        <li><b>3 → 40+</b><span>providers behind one API</span><em>Growth</em></li>
        <li><b>1 API</b><span>instead of one integration per provider</span><em>Primary</em></li>
        <li><b>SDK or API</b><span>two ways for teams and clients to integrate</span><em>Access</em></li>
        <li><b>Our gateway</b><span>one of the providers behind it</span><em>Reuse</em></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="fine dnote">Draft note: 40+ is Drake’s own count; any measured time savings are still to be confirmed.</p>
    </section>
    <div class="w cgrid">
      <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <p>On this page</p>
        <a href="#a3-context">Context</a><a href="#a3-goals">Goals and measures</a><a href="#a3-role">Role and constraints</a><a href="#a3-options">Options considered</a><a href="#a3-decisions">Key decisions</a><a href="#a3-tradeoffs">Trade-offs</a><a href="#a3-execution">How it shipped</a><a href="#a3-lessons">What I’d carry forward</a>
      </nav>
      <div class="prose">
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a3-context" data-sec>
          <h2>Context</h2>
          <p>Each payment service provider (PSP) has its own, unique integration. Every internal team and every client system had to integrate with each provider one by one, and every new provider meant doing it all again.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a3-goals" data-sec>
          <h2>Goals and measures</h2>
          <ul class="cs-list">
            <li>One simplified API that internal teams and outside clients integrate once</li>
            <li>Access to any provider behind it, through an SDK or the direct API</li>
            <li>Cut integration time for internal teams</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="cs-tw">
            <table class="cs-tbl">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Measure</th>
                  <th scope="col">Role</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>Integration time for internal teams</td>
                  <td><span class="cs-pill k-primary">Primary</span></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Providers available behind the API</td>
                  <td><span class="cs-pill k-secondary">Secondary</span></td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a3-role" data-sec>
          <h2>Role and constraints</h2>
          <p>I designed the orchestration layer and owned which providers it supported. Teams tracking client and market needs helped me prioritize providers, and we added them one by one, in priority order.</p>
          <ul class="cs-list">
            <li>Ran in parallel with the rest of the platform roadmap</li>
            <li>Every provider differed in authentication, callbacks and settlement formats</li>
            <li>Compliance requirements varied from provider to provider</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a3-options" data-sec>
          <h2>Options considered</h2>
          <div class="cs-opts">
            <div class="cs-opt v-rejected"><span class="cs-v"><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4.5 4.5l7 7m0-7l-7 7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>Rejected</span><b>Keep integrating each provider separately</b>
              <p>Every team repeated the work for every provider.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="cs-opt v-chosen"><span class="cs-v"><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3.5 8.5l3 3 6-7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Chosen</span><b>An abstraction layer with one simplified API</b>
              <p>Integrate once, use any provider behind it.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a3-decisions" data-sec>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <div class="cs-decs">
            <div class="cs-dec"><span>Decision 1</span><b>Start with three providers</b>
              <p>Launched with our own payment gateway and two outside PSPs before adding any more.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="cs-dec"><span>Decision 2</span><b>One API, two ways in</b>
              <p>Offered as an SDK or a direct API, so internal teams and clients could integrate the way that fit them.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="cs-dec"><span>Decision 3</span><b>Add providers by priority</b>
              <p>Grew to more than 40 providers, one at a time, as client and market needs came up.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a3-tradeoffs" data-sec>
          <h2>Trade-offs</h2>
          <div class="cs-trades">
            <div class="cs-trade"><b>A common API</b><span>over</span><s>Every provider’s unique features</s>
              <p>Some provider-specific features weren’t exposed, in exchange for one integration that works with every provider.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a3-execution" data-sec>
          <h2>How it shipped</h2>
          <ol class="cs-steps">
            <li><span>2023</span>Zero to one: the orchestration layer and its API, launched with our own gateway and two outside PSPs</li>
            <li><span>2023 onward</span>One to 100: providers added one by one, in priority order, to more than 40</li>
          </ol>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a3-lessons" data-sec>
          <h2>What I’d carry forward</h2>
          <ul class="cs-list">
            <li>Design the abstraction around the most different provider, not the first one.</li>
            <li>Prioritize providers by client demand, not by how easy they are to integrate.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
